// Calling a primitive method on the wrong kind of value is a compile error.
//
// Regression fixture. The v1 compiled backend picked the method arm from the
// method *name* and then untagged the receiver to a pointer of the kind it
// assumed. A name does not prove a kind: in `fn f(v) { v.keys() }` the receiver
// was only known when f was called. So `v.keys()` on a string dereferenced a
// char* as a dict and killed the process.
//
// v2 checks a function at the types each call gives it, so `f("str")` is
// refused before the program runs, and the try/catch shape v1 used to ask a
// value its type is gone with it. method_type_guard_error.jde pins the refusal.
//
// What is left here is the other half of the old guard: the real methods still
// work on the kinds that have them.
let d = {"k": "v"}
print(d.keys())      // [k]
print(d.values())    // [v]
let a = [3, 1, 2]
a.push(4)
a.sort()
print(a)             // [1, 2, 3, 4]
print("  pad  ".trim())          // pad
print("a-b".replace("-", "+"))   // a+b
